WEBVTT

00:05.680 --> 00:10.150
So much of what we'll learn in this course is about the relationship between different numbers, the

00:10.150 --> 00:13.030
kind of relationships we use all the time in real life.

00:13.360 --> 00:19.510
For example, when you buy milk at the grocery store, do you want to pay a higher cost per ounce but

00:19.510 --> 00:22.010
less in total for a smaller size?

00:22.030 --> 00:28.840
Or would you rather pay a lower cost per ounce but more in total for a larger size?

00:29.350 --> 00:34.060
We wrapped up the last lesson group by looking at negative numbers and their relationship to positive

00:34.060 --> 00:34.670
numbers.

00:34.690 --> 00:41.320
So now we want to understand how a pair of numbers like negative two and two or negative seven and seven

00:41.620 --> 00:43.030
are related to one another.

00:43.670 --> 00:48.410
Continuing with that same idea in this section, we'll look at the relationship between numbers like

00:48.410 --> 00:50.030
ten and 15.

00:50.510 --> 00:52.180
How are these two numbers related?

00:52.190 --> 00:58.790
If one of them is equal to five times two and the other one is equal to five times three.

00:59.610 --> 01:04.590
Not only is this idea important for its own sake, it's also a foundational concept that we need to

01:04.590 --> 01:06.780
understand before we get into the next lesson.

01:06.780 --> 01:08.190
Group Fractions.

01:08.550 --> 01:13.890
We'll get started by looking at to visibility, which is about figuring out which numbers divide evenly

01:13.890 --> 01:15.270
into larger numbers.
